@* WHY CascadingAuthenticationState WRAPS THE ROUTER
   <AuthorizeRouteView> and every <AuthorizeView>/[Authorize] in the app read the current user from an
   AuthenticationState that must be CASCADED down the component tree. CascadingAuthenticationState is the provider
   that subscribes to our JwtAuthenticationStateProvider and pushes the ClaimsPrincipal down as a cascading value.
   Without it, authorization components have no state to read and treat everyone as anonymous — or throw. It belongs
   at the very root so it covers every routed page.

   WHY AuthenticationGate SITS BETWEEN THE CASCADE AND THE ROUTER
   The gate does not render the router until the token has actually been read from localStorage. That ordering is
   what makes authorization decisions trustworthy: without it the router evaluates [Authorize] during the window
   where JS interop does not exist yet, sees "anonymous" because nothing has been read, and bounces a signed-in
   user to /login on every refresh. See AuthenticationGate for the full account.

   WHY <NotAuthorized> RENDERS A COMPONENT INSTEAD OF NAVIGATING INLINE
   Calling Navigation.NavigateTo(...) directly in this markup executes during the RENDER pass, which Blazor
   interprets as a framework auth challenge and which then demands IAuthenticationService (part of the server
   cookie-auth stack we do not use) — throwing at runtime. Delegating to <RedirectToLogin>, which navigates from
   OnAfterRender, moves the redirect out of the render pass and fixes it. *@
<CascadingAuthenticationState>
    <AuthenticationGate>
        <Router AppAssembly="typeof(Program).Assembly">
            <Found Context="routeData">
                <AuthorizeRouteView RouteData="routeData" DefaultLayout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)">
                    <NotAuthorized>
                        @* Reached only once the token has been read, so this genuinely means "signed out" and the
                           redirect is unambiguous. *@
                        <RedirectToLogin />
                    </NotAuthorized>
                    <Authorizing>
                        <div class="d-flex justify-center pa-8">
                            <MudProgressCircular Color="Color.Primary" Indeterminate="true" />
                        </div>
                    </Authorizing>
                </AuthorizeRouteView>
                <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="routeData" Selector="h1" />
            </Found>

            @* WHY A <NotFound> SLOT AT ALL
               Without it, a mistyped or stale URL renders the router's default bare "Sorry, there's nothing at this
               address." with no layout, no navigation, and no way back — indistinguishable from a broken app.
               Wrapping the handler in MainLayout keeps the user inside the product with the nav still available.

               It is deliberately NOT wrapped in <AuthorizeRouteView>: a 404 is not an authorization outcome, and an
               anonymous visitor hitting a bad URL should see "no such page", not be bounced through a login they do
               not need. *@
            <NotFound>
                <LayoutView Layout="typeof(Layout.MainLayout)">
                    <NotFoundContent />
                </LayoutView>
            </NotFound>
        </Router>
    </AuthenticationGate>
</CascadingAuthenticationState>
